WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.650
At Slop University, Dr Brynjar Obiora leads the soil survey.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.140
I surveyed soils for twelve years.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.820
I dug the pits, and I drew the lines between the auger holes.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.310
But the ground rarely changes at a line.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:15.860
It changes by degrees.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.210
So who decides where one soil ends?

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:22.010
Dr Obiora has asked that question across a remarkable body of work.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.800
Two hundred and fifty-nine research outputs,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.640
the third most at the University.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.590
And an h-index of seventeen,

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:30.330
the highest in his school.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.940
Five hundred and thirty-eight thousand dollars in seed funding,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.770
to replace the faded chips in the soil colour book,

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.500
one page at a time.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.900
I still take a pencil into the field.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.380
These days, I'm slower to draw the line.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.162
Dig into Dr Obiora's work on our website,

00:00:48.312 --> 00:00:50.102
and follow us on Bluesky.
